WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.620
- I'm about to dissect the
case of "Grey's Anatomy".

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:03.570
But I think I want to do it,

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:04.720
not just medically.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:05.820
We should do it legally.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:07.458
Welcome to the channel, Legal Eagle!

00:00:07.458 --> 00:00:08.291
(cheers)

00:00:08.291 --> 00:00:09.124
- Hey, Legal Eagle's!

00:00:09.124 --> 00:00:09.957
- Bewop!

00:00:09.957 --> 00:00:12.071
(air whooshing)

00:00:12.071 --> 00:00:14.056
(loud crash)

00:00:14.056 --> 00:00:14.889
- Crap.

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:16.500
- [Narrator] Doctors
never mean to screw up

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:17.977
- Crap, crap, crap, crap.
(machines beeping)

00:00:17.977 --> 00:00:21.310
- "Crap, crap, crap, crap"
is the last thing you want

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:23.340
to hear in a surgical room.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.320
- If I saw a transcript that
said "crap, crap, crap."

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.510
I would see dollar sign,
(Dr. Mike laughs)

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:28.870
dollar sign, dollar sign.
(cash register dinging)

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:32.500
- It's not like we want to hurt
anyone, but sometimes we do.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.440
Sometimes we make mistakes.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.290
- And then the lawyers make it right.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.020
- I'm looking for Dr. Torres.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:39.853
- Yes. Hi.

00:00:39.853 --> 00:00:42.047
Great. You're being sued.

00:00:42.047 --> 00:00:43.270
(both laugh)

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:44.352
- Is that how it happens?

00:00:44.352 --> 00:00:45.460
- Sort of.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.823
In a medical malpractice
situation like this,

00:00:47.823 --> 00:00:50.530
you obviously have to
start the lawsuit somehow.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:53.045
And the way you do that is
called process of service.

00:00:53.045 --> 00:00:56.810
And if you didn't know
where the person was

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:57.770
or where they worked

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:00.010
or you didn't know who their lawyer was,

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:01.840
then you would have to hire someone called

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:04.506
the process server to
actually physically hand you--

00:01:04.506 --> 00:01:06.430
- Is that the person that
like throws it at you

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:07.934
and says "You got served"?

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:08.767
- It can.

00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:09.990
'Cause I've seen that in movies.

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:10.823
They throw it and it's like,

00:01:10.823 --> 00:01:11.656
"Oh I got served!"

00:01:11.656 --> 00:01:12.489
What does that mean?

00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:14.650
- You need have a competent
adult who was able

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:17.630
to verify that this is the
person who is being sued.

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:18.860
- So throwing it in a door...

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:20.570
- That is not best practices, no.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:22.430
- [Travis] Ready to build me a new hip?

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:24.350
- [Man] He needs to be able to do this...

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.457
see, right there.

00:01:26.457 --> 00:01:27.890
Can a new hip do that?

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:28.723
- More importantly,

00:01:28.723 --> 00:01:29.820
will he be doing it by December?

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.230
He's got Olympic qualifiers coming up.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:32.670
- You mentioned a crash last year.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:33.503
- They said it gave me arthritis.

00:01:33.503 --> 00:01:35.210
- [Man] You can't win
the gold with arthritis.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:36.280
- I want the Peterson hip.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.560
- Would one hard crash cause arthritis

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.170
for the rest of his young career?

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:42.510
- It sounds off.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.190
The only thing I can imagine is

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.530
if you have a significant
loss of cartilage

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.890
or potentially a labral tear,

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:50.630
but there's other
procedures that you can go

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:52.040
about from an orthopedic standpoint

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:53.550
rather than just replacing the hip.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:54.730
Also, what they're asking

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:56.860
of the doctors is not practical, at all.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:58.540
To make promises that
he's going to be able

00:01:58.540 --> 00:01:59.760
to do a move,

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.860
that promise on recovery time,

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.770
none of this is ever guaranteed

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:06.240
because in medicine you don't
even know what's gonna happen

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:07.710
until you open a patient up.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.220
Especially in a specific case like this.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:10.440
This is hyper-specific.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.320
He's not a run of the mill
joint replacement patient.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:14.153
- It's funny you say that

00:02:14.153 --> 00:02:15.960
because one of the first
cases you learn about

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.530
in law school is called Hawkins vs. McGee.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:20.680
You might have heard of it
colloquially 'cause it's called

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.320
the "hairy hand" case.
- Ooh.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:23.860
- Where in the turn of the century,

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:24.920
a doctor

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.510
had a patient who had a hand condition,

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.430
I think his hand was burned,

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:31.260
and the doctor promised

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.810
that not only would he have
a fully functional hand

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:36.733
but it would be a hundred
percent recovered.

00:02:36.733 --> 00:02:39.110
- Okay.
- And he had a skin graft.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:40.110
And as a result

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:42.840
he was not given the a
hundred percent normal hand

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.378
but a hand that was functional, but hairy.

00:02:45.378 --> 00:02:47.990
(Dr. Mike laughs)
And so, the lawsuit was,

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:50.830
can you recover damages for the breach

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:52.380
of the promise that was given

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.640
when he was promised a
hundred percent normal hand

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.060
and given a hairy hand?

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:57.620
And the answer is, yes.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:00.210
You have to value whatever
the difference is in money

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:02.250
between a normal hand and a hairy hand.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:03.490
- Buddy of mine got this one,

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:05.460
he was back on the mountain in no time.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:06.960
This is what I need.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:07.793
- Okay.

00:03:07.793 --> 00:03:08.760
Even if I did agree

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.170
that a hip replacement is
the right course of treatment

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:14.300
I've never even used the
Peterson resurfacing hip joint.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:17.310
I doubt you or your team
want you to be my first.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:18.240
- Yes!

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.110
No experience whatsoever.

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:22.460
Barreling forward without
doing the research

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:23.860
and making specific promises.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:24.693
This is...

00:03:24.693 --> 00:03:25.940
- A lot of patients in general,

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:28.527
treat medicine as if it's
like a shopping thing.

00:03:28.527 --> 00:03:30.230
"I want X."

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.950
When in reality sometimes
"X" isn't what's best

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:34.190
for the patient.

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:35.360
And sometimes it puts you

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:36.430
in this murky position

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:37.500
where the patient feels like they're not

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:38.930
getting what they want.

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:40.020
You're not giving it to them

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.820
because you think it's not
in their best interest.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.040
And then they do something like,

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.500
leave a review for you
on one of these websites.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:48.110
And as a result, you
can't even comment on that

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.400
'cause legally you're
breaking patient privacy

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:51.237
if you comment on that.

00:03:51.237 --> 00:03:52.700
- Oh, I never thought about that.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:54.570
- Yeah.
- That's absolutely right.

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:55.850
- I'll research the joint.

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:56.683
I'll learn what I can.

00:03:56.683 --> 00:03:58.830
I'm not making any promises.

00:03:58.830 --> 00:03:59.950
- [Travis] I'm not worried.

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:01.210
- Despite the fact that she appears

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:03.024
to have been actually
making some promises,

00:04:03.024 --> 00:04:05.700
it is obviously good that she is going

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:06.560
to do some research here.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:08.270
It's not necessarily dispositive

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:10.313
that a doctor hasn't
done a procedure before

00:04:10.313 --> 00:04:12.490
- Sure
and is therefore negligent

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:13.940
based whatever outcome happens.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:15.459
But often in cases like this,

00:04:15.459 --> 00:04:17.880
at least the manufacturer has an interest

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:19.610
in making sure that their own products

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:20.900
are installed correctly.

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:22.050
So, to my knowledge,

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:25.710
medical manufacturers will
give doctors extensive training

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:29.020
so that things are done
correctly on the user end.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:30.135
- They will send in a rep

00:04:30.135 --> 00:04:33.115
during the time of the
procedure to be with us

00:04:33.115 --> 00:04:35.910
and guide us with the
questions that we may have.

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:37.330
And they're really well-trained

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:39.870
because a lot of the stuff, tech wise,

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:41.490
comes out so soon.

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:44.213
You just have to have
them on the same page.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:46.330
- I'm not settling.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:50.003
Okay.
(Dr. Mike chuckles)

00:04:50.003 --> 00:04:52.253
- Okay, you've convinced me.

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:54.670
- I tried.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:56.260
- And there are things
to remember tomorrow.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:57.550
Let the jury see your face.

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:01.230
- Wait, tomorrow? What?
- That's... how fast is that?

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:02.650
- That's ridiculous.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:04.440
This is a conversation
that they would be having

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:07.400
on day one, on day two, on day three,

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.100
this would be a multi-year process.

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:10.210
- Let the jury see your face.

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:11.043
You wear a suit.

00:05:11.043 --> 00:05:12.040
Dark, not bright.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.170
Skirt, pants, minimal jewelry,

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:15.069
heels, pantyhose--

00:05:15.069 --> 00:05:16.250
- Pantyhose?

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:17.890
- Professional but feminine.

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:19.935
You still have your wedding ring?

00:05:19.935 --> 00:05:20.768
- Yes.

00:05:20.768 --> 00:05:21.740
- Wear it.

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:24.160
Juries respond better to
marry women with families.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:26.390
- Ooh, that's a little misogynistic there.

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:27.640
I mean, so there is,

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:28.850
there's a whole science

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:31.210
to presenting yourself
in front of the jury.

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:32.660
This is going a little bit crazy

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:33.930
in terms of the specifics.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:35.710
I mean, you want to dress nicely.

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:36.790
There are whole memes

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:39.950
on the internet about Tekashi 69 showing

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:42.560
up to court with like
tattoos all over his face,

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:44.010
his tie like down to here

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.220
and like his shirt out and stuff.

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:48.690
So you don't want to show up
to court looking like that.

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:51.130
Being so specific about
what you're wearing.

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:51.970
She's a doctor, she's--

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:53.430
- The colors, does it matter?

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:54.790
I can't imagine...
- Not really.

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:57.363
Maybe on the extreme margins.

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:02.100
- You ready?

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:02.933
- Sure.

00:06:02.933 --> 00:06:03.790
- Okay. Okay.

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:06.600
As someone who knows a fair
bit about men's fashion,

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:07.433
what do you notice

00:06:07.433 --> 00:06:09.223
about the shirt that this guy is wearing.

00:06:09.223 --> 00:06:13.210
- It's a different color
collar and probably cuffs.

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:14.043
- Yeah.

00:06:14.043 --> 00:06:16.040
So, he's wearing what's
called a contrast collar

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:17.420
and French cuffs.
- Yes.

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:19.970
- Which is, if I had to choose...

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:21.090
the most pretentious thing

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:23.280
you could possibly wear with a suit,

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:24.550
I would say contrast collars.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:26.300
Now, there's nothing wrong with that.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:29.830
You know, if you're a tobacco tycoon maybe

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:31.740
and you're drinking a
mint julep on a patio,

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:33.050
contrast collar's great.

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:35.630
I believe that there's only
one proper color for a shirt,

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:37.560
which is white. But that--
- White, okay. (laughs)

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:40.135
It makes shopping a lot easier, right?

00:06:40.135 --> 00:06:42.885
(dramatic music)

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:46.540
An above the knee amputation

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:48.000
is usually a more aggressive one.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.300
If you can, you always
try and go below the knee

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:51.770
for stability purposes.

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:53.340
Also, there's a lot of blood vessels here

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:54.690
that are really dangerous.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:57.500
So, most patients that I
see who have amputations,

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:01.660
usually to like diabetes
complications, gangrene,

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:03.240
are below the knee amputations.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:05.530
It's rare for me, at
least in primary care,

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:06.910
to see above the knee amputations.

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:10.620
- I imagine you want to save
something below the knees

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:13.440
so that the prosthesis is
more functional that way.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:14.769
- Yep, exactly.

00:07:14.769 --> 00:07:15.991
(door closes)

00:07:15.991 --> 00:07:17.400
(dramatic music)

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:20.133
- Okay, you do rise
when the judge comes in.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:23.440
- Would if I could.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:24.490
- So that is great.

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:27.100
There's a whole science and pseudoscience

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:30.840
to making your client
presentable and human

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:32.580
in front of the jury.
- Okay.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:34.750
- And you're technically
not allowed to talk

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:35.583
to the jury,

00:07:35.583 --> 00:07:38.570
except in opening statement
and closing argument.

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:40.500
- Okay.
- So, anything you can do

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:42.440
to humanize your clients,
- Wow.

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:43.273
the better.

00:07:43.273 --> 00:07:45.860
- And notice he's not
wearing a tie.. makes sense

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:47.240
and he's a young guy...

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:49.890
humanizes him a little bit.

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:52.037
But to kinda get that jab in and say,

00:07:52.037 --> 00:07:54.157
"Oh gosh, I can't stand up

00:07:54.157 --> 00:07:55.187
"because of this horrible thing

00:07:55.187 --> 00:07:56.470
"that the doctor did to me."

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:58.150
That's really smart.
- Driving home the point.

00:07:58.150 --> 00:07:59.580
- Driving home the point.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:01.790
It's not gonna show up on
any transcript, anywhere.

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:03.120
- Now, serving as a juror

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:05.280
in a medical malpractice case, isn't easy.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:08.220
I don't know about
y'all, but I'm no doctor.

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:09.470
Hell, I barely made it

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:11.500
through high school biology.
(jury laughs softly)

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:12.700
- Okay, fine.

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:15.740
- But you are going to get
a crash course education

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:19.410
in orthopedic medicine and
postoperative infections.

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:22.060
And you're going to hear
words like "pseudomonas"

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:23.417
and "emboli".

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:26.170
Don't let that intimidate you.

00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:27.700
- So, the sort of "aw shucks" thing.

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:29.480
Like, "I'm not a doctor."

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:30.690
Blah, blah, blah.

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:31.880
That can be good.

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:36.510
But you want to pair that
with making the defense seem

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:37.840
like they're the ones that are trying

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:39.620
to overly complicate things.
- Got it.

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:42.510
And that you're the speaker of truth.

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:44.340
You're going to tell them
exactly what happened

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:45.690
'cause you're not hiding anything.

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:46.780
In an opening statement like this,

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:48.487
what you would want to say is,

00:08:48.487 --> 00:08:52.010
"You're gonna hear a
lot of medical jargon.

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:54.670
The defense wants you to be confused

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:55.810
about these things",

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:57.660
but you want to transition to your theme.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:08:59.500
The one thing you want
the jury to remember

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:01.330
throughout the entire case.

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:02.800
The lone star that they can hold onto.

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:03.817
And you say,

00:09:03.817 --> 00:09:05.710
"Despite all this complicated stuff,

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:07.000
and we're gonna explain it to you,

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:10.240
the one thing you need
to remember is... this."

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:13.730
- Dr. Torres agreed to perform a surgery

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:16.010
she did not fully understand.

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:17.790
We will establish that Dr. Torres

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:20.450
made a series of faulty decisions.

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:23.110
Eventually costing Mr. Travis Reed,

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:27.000
a thriving, healthy Olympic athlete,

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:27.913
his legs.

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:31.060
And we will establish that all

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:34.200
of this started because
of one careless mistake.

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:35.530
- So, that's not bad.

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:37.050
It's a little argumentative.

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:39.530
An opening statement is not
like a closing argument,

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:40.680
where you're allowed to make whatever

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:41.670
arguments you want.
- Okay.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:43.540
An opening statement is there

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:45.420
so that you can tell the jury

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:46.560
what is going to happen.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:48.270
What evidence is going to come out

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:49.863
based on your extensive discovery,

00:09:49.863 --> 00:09:50.880
that you have done.
- Sure.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.580
- What I don't like
about this is that he has

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:55.777
put the stakes too high.

00:09:55.777 --> 00:09:58.997
"This doctor made one huge mistake.

00:09:58.997 --> 00:10:02.380
"And this doctor did something
she didn't understand."

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:03.886
If I was the defense counsel--

00:10:03.886 --> 00:10:05.200
- You would just disprove that, he loses.

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:06.033
Exactly.

00:10:06.033 --> 00:10:07.397
In my closing argument I would say,

00:10:07.397 --> 00:10:10.020
"Remember when the
plaintiff's attorney said,

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:11.577
"'The doctor didn't understand that'?

00:10:11.577 --> 00:10:14.287
"Well, you heard from
the doctor that she did

00:10:14.287 --> 00:10:15.657
"a hundred hours of research,

00:10:15.657 --> 00:10:17.467
"corresponded with the manufacturer

00:10:17.467 --> 00:10:19.077
"and brought the manufacturing rep

00:10:19.077 --> 00:10:20.817
"out to participate in surgery.

00:10:20.817 --> 00:10:22.500
"She knew what she was doing."

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:25.570
Is it true that in
medical malpractice cases,

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:27.086
unlike the burden of proof

00:10:27.086 --> 00:10:29.490
being beyond a reasonable doubt,

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:31.230
it just has to be more likely than not?

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:32.500
- That's exactly correct.
- Okay.

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:34.690
- And that is the case
with all civil cases.

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:36.710
It's called the
preponderance of the evidence

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:37.870
standard.

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:39.150
- All right, let's start
closing. Irrigation, please.

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:40.420
- Wait, the sponge count is off,

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:41.390
we're missing one sponge.

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:42.230
- Are you kidding me?

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:43.113
- You can't close until we find it.

00:10:43.113 --> 00:10:44.920
- So this is true.

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:46.600
We do a very careful sponge count

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:49.450
before and after the
procedure prior to closing

00:10:49.450 --> 00:10:50.500
because there have been cases,

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:51.510
as you're well aware,

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:53.080
of sponges being left in patients,

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:54.300
tools being left in patients.

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:55.970
In fact, I remember when we were operating

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:59.310
we would have on the wall
all the sponges that we've,

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:00.640
were gonna use for that case.

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:02.230
So there would be like
30 sponges on the wall

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:03.270
in little pockets,

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:04.452
almost like the shoe things, you know?

00:11:04.452 --> 00:11:05.330
- Right, yeah that makes sense.

00:11:05.330 --> 00:11:07.290
- And then as we take them out,

00:11:07.290 --> 00:11:08.620
we put them back in.

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:10.060
And we've made certain adjustments where

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:12.400
before we would just use
sponges by themselves.

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:14.610
Now, they have an attached plastic ring

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:16.080
that makes them easier to spot.

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:18.580
You could also leave that
plastic ring outside of the body

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:20.040
so you know to take the sponge out.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:20.873
- Oh!

00:11:20.873 --> 00:11:21.706
- So, there's been a lot of adjustments

00:11:21.706 --> 00:11:22.740
to improve here

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:24.380
but clearly it's still happens sometimes.

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:26.610
- Are you saying that Dr. Torres

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:30.450
knew she left a sponge in Mr. Reed's body

00:11:30.450 --> 00:11:31.330
and she didn't care?

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:33.380
- [Jo] No, of course she cared but--

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:35.552
- No, but still she knowingly left--

00:11:35.552 --> 00:11:37.280
- What?!
- Who is this on the stand?

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:38.300
- Exactly!

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:41.030
This is one of the nurses or doctors

00:11:41.030 --> 00:11:42.340
in the hospital.

00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:46.340
So, the plaintiff as
apparently his first witness,

00:11:46.340 --> 00:11:48.183
has called a hostile witness.

00:11:48.183 --> 00:11:49.016
(Dr. Mike laughs)

00:11:49.016 --> 00:11:50.430
That is insane.

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:52.260
Juries, like all humans

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:55.370
have the primacy and latency effect.

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:57.140
So, they're gonna remember
what they heard first

00:11:57.140 --> 00:11:58.400
and they're gonna remember
what they heard last.

00:11:58.400 --> 00:11:59.233
- Yes.

00:11:59.233 --> 00:12:01.030
- So, using that to your advantage

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:02.100
at this point to the stand,

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:04.530
you would call either
the plaintiff himself

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:07.450
to tell the entire sad story
of what happened to him.

00:12:07.450 --> 00:12:09.640
Or you call your expert,

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:11.620
who is going to explain,
- On your side.

00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:13.583
on your side, all of the medical stuff,

00:12:13.583 --> 00:12:15.790
from the secondary material.

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:18.660
You would never call a witness

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:20.150
that is basically

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:21.410
on the other side.
- Yeah.

00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:23.100
- The patient was running out of time.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:24.330
He would have--

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:25.743
- Hun, hun. It's a yes or no question.

00:12:25.743 --> 00:12:26.929
- (both exclaim in shock) Hun hun?

00:12:26.929 --> 00:12:28.470
(laughs loud)

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:30.800
- I've got a boy in college, good kid.

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:32.200
And I went to visit him last month

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:34.490
and you would not believe
the sponge I found

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:36.130
in his sink. I mean there's no tellin'--

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:36.963
- Ask a question.

00:12:36.963 --> 00:12:38.060
- Objection, irrelevant.

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:39.050
- Yes, that was irrelevant.

00:12:39.050 --> 00:12:42.060
But that's like the fifth
most important objection

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:42.893
to make here?
(Dr. Mike laughs)

00:12:42.893 --> 00:12:43.726
He's just telling a story.

00:12:43.726 --> 00:12:45.017
"I got this kid in college.

00:12:45.017 --> 00:12:46.187
"He's a real mess.

00:12:46.187 --> 00:12:47.980
"He left a sponge in the sink."

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:50.100
The only thing you're allowed
to do, during examination,

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:51.540
is ask questions. That's it.

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:52.373
- Well, let's fast forward

00:12:52.373 --> 00:12:53.770
to the next time you saw Mr. Reed.

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:56.080
- About two weeks later.

00:12:56.080 --> 00:12:56.990
- And how was he then?

00:12:56.990 --> 00:12:58.710
Was he... good?

00:12:58.710 --> 00:12:59.640
Happy?

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:00.543
Recovering well?

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:04.780
- He had an infection.
(whoosh)

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:05.800
- Hi there.

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:07.440
Travis, I hear you got quite the fever.

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:10.060
- [Jo] Temp's 103, I'm
just starting his workup.

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:10.893
- He's burning up.

00:13:10.893 --> 00:13:13.010
- And she said this is weeks later?

00:13:13.010 --> 00:13:13.843
- Apparently.

00:13:13.843 --> 00:13:15.400
- But weeks later,

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:18.100
she's just seeing him for the first time?

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:19.920
Like, she would see him the next day.

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:21.000
- That sounds negligent.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:22.830
- [Girlfriend] I had to
force him to come in.

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:24.010
- [Callie] How are you feeling Travis?

00:13:24.010 --> 00:13:25.630
- Wait, he went home with a fever?

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:26.550
- No!

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:27.860
- Post-surgical fever can happen.

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:29.340
And there's like, a thing that we remember

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:30.820
in medical school called the five W's.

00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:33.740
Wind, water, walking, wonder drugs...

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:34.820
and they all mean something different.

00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:37.570
Like UTI's, pulmonary
embolism is one of them,

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:39.390
wind being pneumonia.

00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:41.240
Medications that you could
be taking can cause a fever

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:42.530
and you try and rule things out.

00:13:42.530 --> 00:13:44.560
But the fact that he
went home with a fever

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:45.810
and he's 103,

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:47.730
that's a really high fever.

00:13:47.730 --> 00:13:49.680
That should be thoroughly investigated.

00:13:54.257 --> 00:13:55.703
- Is that a murmur?

00:13:55.703 --> 00:13:58.211
- Okay, so they are
listening to the heart.

00:13:58.211 --> 00:14:00.640
- Did they listen to the right side?

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:01.810
- Well, it's funny when you listen

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:02.643
for heart sounds,

00:14:02.643 --> 00:14:03.960
you actually listen starting

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:05.710
on the right side of the body.

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:06.750
And a lot of people,

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:09.210
patients that are medically in tuned

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:12.290
from watching shows or have some curiosity

00:14:12.290 --> 00:14:13.123
they'll say,

00:14:13.123 --> 00:14:14.037
"Doctor, you know, the heart's

00:14:14.037 --> 00:14:15.250
"on the left side."
(laughs)

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:16.083
I'm like,

00:14:16.083 --> 00:14:18.250
"Well, there are blood vessels that come

00:14:18.250 --> 00:14:19.780
off the heart that are on the right side

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:21.330
that we listen to as well."

00:14:21.330 --> 00:14:22.410
The pulmonary artery.

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:23.600
So you want to listen there

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:25.520
and then move your way, make your way,

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:27.650
aorta, listen to the
chambers of the heart.

00:14:27.650 --> 00:14:29.490
So, yeah we do listen on the right side

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:30.390
to hear the heart.

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:33.013
- That looks bad.
(Jo in the background) Oh wow.

00:14:33.013 --> 00:14:34.420
- That looks like an infected wound.

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:35.300
- What does "oh wow" mean?

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:38.370
Yeah so, when we give medical feedback,

00:14:38.370 --> 00:14:39.990
when we listen to a patient's lungs,

00:14:39.990 --> 00:14:41.060
when we look at wounds,

00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:44.131
we tend not to exclaim our feelings.

00:14:44.131 --> 00:14:45.090
(laughs)

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:45.923
So, we don't say,

00:14:45.923 --> 00:14:46.960
"That sounds scary.

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:48.280
That's good. That's bad..."

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:50.320
It's either normal or abnormal

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:51.153
and you keep it at that.

00:14:51.153 --> 00:14:52.630
- I don't want you to worry.

00:14:52.630 --> 00:14:53.954
The wound looks a little infected--

00:14:53.954 --> 00:14:55.260
- A little infected?!

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:56.093
- Okay?
- Yeah

00:14:57.233 --> 00:14:58.680
- I mean, he looks like
a zombie from my eyes.

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:00.580
What I'm seeing right here is all kinds

00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:04.280
of reasons why the hospital
could have not only contributed

00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:05.510
to the initial negligence

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:07.910
but committed its own
malpractice, as well.

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:10.440
Which is, that this
patient is being sent home

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:11.360
too early.
- Yes.

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:13.700
- This patient is not being checked

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:15.160
on regularly.
- Yeah.

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:16.390
- And to the extent that they are checking

00:15:16.390 --> 00:15:17.223
on this patient,

00:15:17.223 --> 00:15:19.650
they're not examining the wound

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:22.210
where the surgery took place.
- (laughs) Yeah, exactly.

00:15:22.210 --> 00:15:24.208
That wound did not get infected today.

00:15:24.208 --> 00:15:25.160
(both laugh)

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:26.950
That's been an infection
that's been festering.

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:28.840
And generally speaking,
after you have surgery

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:30.850
you have not only
follow-ups with the surgeon,

00:15:30.850 --> 00:15:33.200
you have follow-ups with
your primary care doctor.

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:34.960
All of which times we do vitals,

00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:35.950
we check a wound.

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:38.630
In fact, anytime a patient
now is hospitalized.

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:41.340
We have one of our nurses
call the patient the next day

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:42.790
to make sure they understand the medicines

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:43.623
they should be taking,

00:15:43.623 --> 00:15:44.930
what symptoms are they experiencing?

00:15:44.930 --> 00:15:46.603
And if anything is off,
they come right back.

00:15:46.603 --> 00:15:47.920
- What about the murmur?

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:49.430
Could the infection have
traveled to his heart?

00:15:49.430 --> 00:15:50.263
- His vitals were stable.

00:15:50.263 --> 00:15:51.730
So, first we gotta clean
out that joint, okay?

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:52.860
We can't afford any delay.

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:54.770
- See? Another thing that's untrue.

00:15:54.770 --> 00:15:57.100
How does she know the
wound is not just infected

00:15:57.100 --> 00:15:58.640
versus the joint is infected?

00:15:58.640 --> 00:15:59.473
- Well, at this point,

00:15:59.473 --> 00:16:00.880
they might as well just
paint a dollar sign

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:01.787
on top of the hospital.
- Exactly.

00:16:01.787 --> 00:16:03.283
- And this doctor might as well be

00:16:03.283 --> 00:16:04.580
just taking wads of cash

00:16:04.580 --> 00:16:06.080
and lighting it on fire.
- Honestly, I think if,

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:07.770
you can probably create your own law firm

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:09.430
that just targets their patients,

00:16:09.430 --> 00:16:10.940
'cause you'd be very well off.

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:12.610
- Post-op infections are very common.

00:16:12.610 --> 00:16:13.828
They happen all the time.

00:16:13.828 --> 00:16:15.072
- Is that your professional opinion?

00:16:15.072 --> 00:16:16.111
- It is.
- I see.

00:16:16.111 --> 00:16:17.690
And in your professional opinion,

00:16:17.690 --> 00:16:19.960
is it also common for a post-op infection

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:22.020
to result in a double amputation?

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:23.080
Does that happen all the time too?

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:24.950
- You don't know what she's
going to say right there.

00:16:24.950 --> 00:16:25.877
She might say,
- Yes.

00:16:25.877 --> 00:16:27.057
"In a situation like this,

00:16:27.057 --> 00:16:28.097
"where this went wrong,

00:16:28.097 --> 00:16:31.167
"his heart blew up, he had a lung problem.

00:16:31.167 --> 00:16:33.207
"Yeah. That's exactly what
I would expect to see.

00:16:33.207 --> 00:16:35.047
"And these doctors did a fantastic job.

00:16:35.047 --> 00:16:36.430
"That's completely impeccable."

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:37.820
- What would he have done in that case?

00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:39.262
- Thank you, Your Honor. I
have no further questions.

00:16:39.262 --> 00:16:40.095
(both laugh loud)

00:16:40.095 --> 00:16:40.928
- Why didn't you page me sooner?

00:16:40.928 --> 00:16:42.260
We just heard the murmur this morning.

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:43.093
- This morning?

00:16:43.093 --> 00:16:43.926
- Yeah.

00:16:43.926 --> 00:16:44.759
- Look at this growth.

00:16:44.759 --> 00:16:45.840
You knew he was a heart patient.

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:46.680
This didn't concern you?

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:49.240
- Of course it did.
That's why you're here.

00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:50.073
So what did we do?

00:16:50.073 --> 00:16:51.840
- The infection is
eating away at his graft.

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:53.380
- So, not only is having a fever

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:55.460
'cause his joint is infected,

00:16:55.460 --> 00:16:58.040
which she knew somehow
without doing any diagnosis,

00:16:58.040 --> 00:17:00.570
but his heart valve also
has an infection on it.

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:03.750
Vegetations means it has
actual bacterial growth on it.

00:17:03.750 --> 00:17:07.160
And she's only performing
the surgery on his leg,

00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:08.220
but not on the heart?

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:11.940
- Dr. Torres? You need to see this.

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:14.040
- I was changing his compression
socks and I saw this.

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:15.260
His leg's gray, cold.

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:16.093
I can't find a pulse.

00:17:16.093 --> 00:17:17.470
- Why aren't you checking all the time!

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:18.450
- Yeah, exactly.

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:20.610
So, when a patient is laying in a hospital

00:17:20.610 --> 00:17:22.100
they're not just passively laying there.

00:17:22.100 --> 00:17:23.670
The reason they're in the hospital is

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:25.870
because there are wound
checks every few hours,

00:17:25.870 --> 00:17:28.070
neuro checks, the residents come in

00:17:28.070 --> 00:17:29.560
and round on the patient every morning.

00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:31.200
Look at the wound, look at the leg.

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:32.940
- And it seems like,

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:35.980
an area where there's
already been some problems,

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:37.770
you probably don't want to hide them

00:17:37.770 --> 00:17:39.320
behind compression socks.

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:40.153
- Yes, exactly.

00:17:40.153 --> 00:17:41.100
- Okay. Where's Whitney?

00:17:41.100 --> 00:17:42.360
- I told her she had time to grab lunch

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:43.524
before Travis woke up.

00:17:43.524 --> 00:17:44.550
All right? You need to go
find her and tell her--

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:45.450
- That's an ultrasound,

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:46.960
portable ultrasound machine that we use

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:48.880
to hear blood flow.

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:50.780
And the reality of it...

00:17:50.780 --> 00:17:52.980
you don't need that for his leg.

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:55.990
You can see that it's
not getting circulation.

00:17:55.990 --> 00:17:56.823
- Okay. I've got to go in

00:17:56.823 --> 00:17:58.158
and do an embolectomy on the spot.

00:17:58.158 --> 00:17:58.991
- Yep there's a problem.

00:17:58.991 --> 00:17:59.920
- So an embolectomy,

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:02.330
like they're saying that a
blood clot has now traveled

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:03.210
to his arteries.

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:04.980
I can't, I can't even follow this.

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:07.160
There's nothing, there's no medical sense

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:08.760
or rhyme or reason.
- Doesn't that presume

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:12.420
that a blood clot has flown down to his...

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:13.800
- Arterial system now.

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:14.700
- in his legs.

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:16.711
- Yeah, which means it did a full circle.

00:18:16.711 --> 00:18:17.698
(laughs)

00:18:17.698 --> 00:18:18.730
- If I don't try this embolectomy

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:19.563
he could lose his leg.

00:18:19.563 --> 00:18:20.720
- Well he can live without a leg.

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:22.130
He can't live without a functioning heart.

00:18:22.130 --> 00:18:23.170
- Look, if you do the heart tomorrow,

00:18:23.170 --> 00:18:24.780
it gives him an extra day on antibiotics.

00:18:24.780 --> 00:18:25.840
Right? He'll be more stable.

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:27.800
And the heart surgery will be less risky.

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:30.610
Christina, this is Travis
Reed we're talking about!

00:18:30.610 --> 00:18:31.570
Let me try to save his leg.

00:18:31.570 --> 00:18:33.500
- Does the course of treatment
change if you're dealing

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:34.740
with a famous person?

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:35.690
- It doesn't change that.

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:37.410
But it does depend on patient need.

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:40.560
Like if a patient's job is...

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:44.460
intimately tied to a specific
function of their body,

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:45.450
we will work harder

00:18:45.450 --> 00:18:46.640
to save that body part.
- Ah, I see.

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:48.040
- Yeah. So there is some truth to that.

00:18:48.040 --> 00:18:51.100
You may have seen musicians
play their instruments

00:18:51.100 --> 00:18:52.740
during brain surgery.

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:54.960
'Cause they want to make
sure they don't do any damage

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:56.697
to something that's really
important to that person.

00:18:56.697 --> 00:18:58.290
- Whoa! That is cool.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:18:58.290 --> 00:18:59.123
- What's his temp?

00:18:59.123 --> 00:19:00.340
- Oh, he was febrile over night,

00:19:00.340 --> 00:19:01.857
but his white count's stable.

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:06.400
- What's going on?
- Travis?

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:07.250
- What aren't you telling me?

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:08.870
- What's wrong?

00:19:08.870 --> 00:19:10.060
(loud whoosh)

00:19:10.060 --> 00:19:11.350
- That's full on necrosis.

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:13.150
So, here's the weird situation.

00:19:13.150 --> 00:19:14.130
- Why aren't checking on him?

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:16.050
- Why is he the one pointing that out?

00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:17.870
And why are they so casually strolling in

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:18.860
like in the middle of the day?

00:19:18.860 --> 00:19:20.290
He's basically throwing more clots

00:19:20.290 --> 00:19:23.007
and the fact that they
waited to do his heart

00:19:23.007 --> 00:19:25.070
(stutters) it's obviously,

00:19:25.070 --> 00:19:25.913
she's guilty.

00:19:27.740 --> 00:19:28.573
This is a civil suit.

00:19:28.573 --> 00:19:29.560
She's not guilty, she's liable.

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:30.816
- Liable
- And I think so is

00:19:30.816 --> 00:19:31.660
the hospital here.
- Yeah, agreed.

00:19:31.660 --> 00:19:35.510
And notice where his
necrosis was starting from,

00:19:35.510 --> 00:19:36.400
below the knee below.
- Below the knee.

00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:37.310
- Why did they go above?

00:19:37.310 --> 00:19:40.350
- Oh man... that's unfortunate.

